e="description" content="It is estimated that half of all individuals who suffer a stroke are unable to walk immediately afterwards. Stroke survivors often face challenges in regaining their mobility and independence, with impaired strength, sensation and balance often being a common consequence of stroke.  In this blog we will discuss how treadmill training is an effective"/><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:site_name" content="Propel Physiotherapy"/><meta property="og:title" content="Treadmill Training to Improve Walking After Stroke - Propel Physiotherapy"/><meta property="og:description" content="It is estimated that half of all individuals who suffer a stroke are unable to walk immediately afterwards. Stroke survivors often face challenges in regaining their mobility and independence, with impaired strength, sensation and balance often being a common consequence of stroke.  In this blog we will discuss how treadmill training is an effective"/><meta property="og:url" content="https://propelphysiotherapy.com/neurological/stroke-physiotherapy/treadmill-training-to-improve-walking-after-stroke/"/><meta property="article:published_time" content="2024-01-10T10:00:14-04:00"/><meta property="article:modified_time" content="2024-07-16T14:19:37-04:00"/><meta name="author" content="Alexander Georgiou"/><meta property="og:image" content="https://propelphysiotherapy.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/treadmill-training-to-improve-walking-after-stroke-propel-physiotherapy.jpg"/><meta property="og:image:width" content="1920"/><meta property="og:image:height" content="1080"/><meta property="og:image:type" content="image/jpeg"/><style id='wp-img-auto-sizes-contain-inline-css' type='text/css'>img:is([sizes=auto i],[sizes^="auto," i]){contain-intrinsic-size:3000px 1500px}
